//? Title: SGD, Momentum and Nesterov: State, Rates and Stability
//? Subtitle: Exact gradient streams, quadratic modes and an explicit minibatch loop
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A gradient tells us a local direction, but a training loop must decide how far to move
and whether to remember past directions. SGD uses the current gradient; momentum keeps
a decaying gradient accumulator; the Nesterov convention here mixes both. We expose
those states with four prescribed gradients, derive scalar quadratic stability, and run
an explicit minibatch fit. Every comparison names its learning rate and momentum; no
universal ordering of optimizers is inferred from one quadratic or one small dataset.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Separate the gradient, its memory and the parameter displacement

The gradient is evaluated at the stored parameter vector before any parameter changes.
With initial velocity zero, the three rules are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{SGD: }&w_{t+1}=w_t-\eta_t g_t,\\
\text{Momentum: }&v_t=\mu v_{t-1}+g_t,\quad w_{t+1}=w_t-\eta_t v_t,\\
\text{Nesterov here: }&v_t=\mu v_{t-1}+g_t,\quad
 w_{t+1}=w_t-\eta_t(g_t+\mu v_t).
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(SGD subtracts the current rate times the gradient. Momentum first accumulates the gradient in a decaying velocity. The Nesterov convention uses the gradient plus momentum times the updated velocity.)

This is TensorFlow's reparameterized ApplyMomentum convention. It is not an extra
forward evaluation at a separately constructed look-ahead point. The stored weights
represent that parameterization. When rates change, the current rate multiplies the
entire direction; velocity stores gradients, not previously rate-scaled displacements.
For constant gradient g, its velocity is \((1-\mu^t)g/(1-\mu)\); for zero new gradients,
old velocity decays rather than vanishing immediately. A larger displacement is not
necessarily a better optimization step. These deterministic examples use Float64.

The editable TikZ source and its rendering logs stay local. Regeneration requires LaTeX
with PGF/TikZ and standalone plus dvisvgm; exports consume the saved figure.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/sgd_updates.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/sgd_momentum_updates.tikz > build/sgd_updates.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/sgd_updates.svg build/sgd_momentum_updates.dvi > build/sgd_updates.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/sgd_updates.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/sgd_updates.svg from local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/sgd_updates.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The optimizer turns the current gradient and stored state into a parameter displacement. The current learning rate is outside the velocity recurrence.)
\alttext(A gradient box feeds a decaying velocity box; separate arrows lead to SGD, momentum and Nesterov update formulas. A previous-state box supplies the remembered velocity.)

>> One scalar quadratic mode explains stability

For \(f(x)=\lambda x^2/2\), let \(a=\eta\lambda\). SGD multiplies x by 1−a,
so strict stability requires 0<a<2. Momentum's state matrix has trace 1+mu−a and
determinant mu. The Nesterov map used here has trace (1+mu)(1−a) and determinant
mu(1−a). Both follow by substituting the updated velocity into the parameter rule.
Their characteristic roots solve

$$
 r^2-\operatorname{tr}(T)r+\det(T)=0.
$$
\alttext(The two state-map eigenvalues are roots of a quadratic whose coefficients are its trace and determinant.)

The largest root modulus is the spectral radius. At fixed rate and curvature it is
below one precisely in the stable interior. For 0<=mu<1, momentum permits
0<a<2(1+mu); this Nesterov convention permits 0<a<2(1+mu)/(1+2mu).
Radius one is a boundary, not convergence. Complex roots yield oscillation, and repeated
roots can produce a transient polynomial factor even when their modulus is below one.
These statements concern fixed positive quadratic curvatures, not global nonconvex or
stochastic convergence. A diagonal quadratic is stable only if all its modes are stable.

`Optimizer` updates leaves in place without recording update operations in autograd.
It retains the parameter list captured at construction. `minimize(loss)` computes all
parameter gradients at the same old state. Momentum/Nesterov use one velocity slot per
parameter element; plain SGD has no velocity vector. State is separate from model weights
and can be reset or checkpointed. Changing only weights does not reset velocity.

> Simple examples

>> Four gradients make the convention observable

Start at zero with gradients (1,1,−1,0), rates (.1,.1,.1,.05) and mu=.9.
The first update is −.1 for SGD/momentum but −.19 for Nesterov. Momentum's third
velocity remains positive (.71) even though that gradient is negative. A zero fourth
gradient still moves a momentum-based parameter. The helper records the real tensor
optimizer's before/after states; it does not generate the trajectory from a formula.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sgd_stream.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>    gradients(4, 1, {1, 1, -1, 0});
  auto                                     plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame                   all;
  const std::vector<dm::nn::OptimizerKind> kinds = {
      dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Sgd, dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Momentum, dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Nesterov};
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors = {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {22, 163, 74}};
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < kinds.size(); ++i) {
    dm::nn::OptimizerConfig c;
    c.kind          = kinds[i];
    auto       t    = dm::nn::optimizer_gradient_trace({0}, gradients, {.1, .1, .1, .05}, c);
    const auto name = dm::nn::optimizer_name(kinds[i]);
    t.add_column("rule", std::vector<std::string>(t.nrows(), name));
    all = i ? all.concat_rows(t) : t;
    plot.lines(
        t.numeric_column("step").values(), t.numeric_column("after").values(), name, colors[i]);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.title("A changing gradient is filtered by state")
      .x_label("update")
      .y_label("parameter after update")
      .y_limits(-.6, .2)
      .size(900, 420)
      .save_svg("build/sgd_stream.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n", all.select({"rule", "step", "rate", "gradient", "velocity", "after"}).to_md(6));
}
```
