| Momentum | 300 | 0.000000425 | 1.917630241e-11 | 0.948683298 | 0.948683298 |
| Nesterov | 300 | 0.000000136 | 9.213203059e-15 | 0.943927963 | 0.000000000 |

\image(figures/sgd_quadratic.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All three methods see the same deterministic full gradients. Fixed-mode spectral radii explain eventual contraction while the plotted paths retain transients.)
\alttext(Two panels show parameter paths in a soft/stiff quadratic and log objective against update. A table reports final soft coordinates, losses and the two analytic mode radii.)

>> The three stability intervals differ

Scan the dimensionless quantity a=rate times curvature at fixed mu=.9. The radius-one
line separates strict asymptotic contraction from instability. Momentum reaches its
boundary at a=3.8, SGD at two, and this Nesterov convention at 3.8/2.8. A wider interval
alone does not imply faster convergence inside it. The Nesterov scalar map at a=1 is
nilpotent: its spectral radius is zero, although its first iterate can overshoot before
reaching zero on the second step.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sgd_stability.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto                               a      = dm::seql(.001, 4.2, 300);
  auto                                     plot   = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB>     colors = {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {22, 163, 74}};
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame                   table;
  const std::vector<dm::nn::OptimizerKind> kinds = {
      dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Sgd, dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Momentum, dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Nesterov};
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < kinds.size(); ++i) {
    std::vector<double> radius;
    for (double value : a.values())
      radius.push_back(dm::nn::optimizer_mode_radius(kinds[i], value, .9));
    const auto name = dm::nn::optimizer_name(kinds[i]);
    plot.lines(a.values(), radius, name, colors[i]);
  }
  plot.abline_h(1)
      .title("Constant quadratic mode, momentum 0.9")
      .x_label("a = learning rate * curvature")
      .y_label("spectral radius")
      .y_limits(0, 8);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.size(900, 440).save_svg("build/sgd_stability.svg");
  table.add_column("rule", std::vector<std::string>{"SGD", "Momentum", "Nesterov"});
  table.add_column("upper_strict_boundary", std::vector<double>{2, 3.8, 3.8 / 2.8});
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(9));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| rule | upper_strict_boundary |
| :--- | ---: |
| SGD | 2.000000000 |
| Momentum | 3.800000000 |
| Nesterov | 1.357142857 |

\image(figures/sgd_stability.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The scalar-mode stability region belongs to a specified update convention, fixed learning rate and fixed curvature.)
\alttext(Three spectral-radius curves are compared with the radius-one line over dimensionless rates. The table lists the three analytic upper stability boundaries.)

> An explicit minibatch training loop

The small deterministic dataset y=2x+1 at x=(−2,−1,0,1,2) has a known exact fit.
Each optimizer gets fresh parameters, the same BatchOrder seed, rate .03, 40 epochs
and batches of at most two, retaining the last one-row batch. Each step uses the mean
squared error of its current batch. This means the trajectory is stochastic with respect
to its shuffled sample order, unlike the quadratic experiments. It is a demonstration
of the loop and state, not an estimate of prediction performance on unseen data. No
extra scaling, optimizer selection or test-set comparison is performed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sgd_minibatches.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto x = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2}),
             y = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-3, -1, 1, 3, 5});
  auto fit = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), curve = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  fit.points({-2, -1, 0, 1, 2}, {-3, -1, 1, 3, 5}, "observations", {31, 41, 55});
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame                   results;
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB>     colors = {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {22, 163, 74}};
  const std::vector<dm::nn::OptimizerKind> kinds  = {
      dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Sgd, dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Momentum, dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Nesterov};
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < kinds.size(); ++k) {
    auto w = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{0}).requires_grad_(),
         b = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{0}).requires_grad_();
    dm::nn::OptimizerConfig c;
    c.kind = kinds[k];
    dm::nn::Optimizer   opt({w, b}, .03, c);
    dm::nn::BatchOrder  batches(5, 2, 7);
    std::vector<double> epoch, loss;
    for (int e = 0; e < 40; ++e) {
      batches.begin_epoch();
      for (auto ids = batches.next(); !ids.empty(); ids = batches.next()) {
        const auto bx = dm::nn::take_rows(x, ids), by = dm::nn::take_rows(y, ids);
        opt.minimize(dm::nn::mean(dm::nn::square(bx * w + b - by)));
      }
      epoch.push_back(e + 1);
      loss.push_back(
          std::log10(std::max(dm::nn::mean(dm::nn::square(x * w + b - y)).item<double>(), 1e-30)));
    }
    const auto name = dm::nn::optimizer_name(kinds[k]);
    fit.lines(
        {-2, 2},
        {-2 * w.item<double>() + b.item<double>(), 2 * w.item<double>() + b.item<double>()},
        name,
        colors[k]);
    curve.lines(epoch, loss, name, colors[k]);
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame row;
    row.add_column("rule", std::vector<std::string>{name});
    row.add_column("slope", std::vector<double>{w.item<double>()});
    row.add_column("intercept", std::vector<double>{b.item<double>()});
    row.add_column("updates", std::vector<double>{double(opt.iterations())});
    row.add_column(
        "mse", std::vector<double>{dm::nn::mean(dm::nn::square(x * w + b - y)).item<double>()});
    results = k ? results.concat_rows(row) : row;
  }
  fit.title("A five-point linear training fit").x_label("x").y_label("y").y_limits(-4, 8);
  curve.title("Training loss after each full epoch")
      .x_label("epoch")
      .y_label("log10 training MSE")
      .y_limits(-16, 6);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(fit).add(curve).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/sgd_minibatches.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n",
      results.to_md(dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions{.digits = 9, .scientific = {"mse"}}));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| rule | slope | intercept | updates | mse |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| SGD | 1.99997742 | 0.999557369 | 120 | 1.969420525e-07 |
| Momentum | 1.98866251 | 0.997185719 | 120 | 2.649975593e-04 |
| Nesterov | 1.99996459 | 1.000007162 | 120 | 2.558858980e-09 |

\image(figures/sgd_minibatches.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The loop gathers each batch, constructs a scalar loss and asks the optimizer to differentiate/update both parameters synchronously. The same shuffled batch stream is supplied to every rule.)
\alttext(Fitted lines are shown with five observations; a second panel shows epoch-end training MSE on a logarithmic scale. The table lists slopes, intercepts, update counts and final training losses.)

> Conclusion

Velocity, learning rate and gradient evaluation must be stated together to define an
optimizer. The prescribed stream reveals the convention directly; quadratic modes give
conditional stability guarantees; and the explicit batch loop shows where gradients,
parameters and optimizer state live. These examples do not establish a universal winner.
The original C++ example remains a runnable target, including its coupled-gradient and
optimizer-reset checks. Early stopping, schedules, clipping and checkpoint restoration
are developed in their own reports.

> References

- Polyak, B. T. (1964). [Some methods of speeding up the convergence of iteration methods|https://doi.org/10.1016/0041-5553(64)90137-5]. ~USSR Computational Mathematics and Mathematical Physics~, 4(5), 1–17.
- TensorFlow authors. [ApplyMomentum|https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/cc/class/tensorflow/ops/apply-momentum]. The accumulated-gradient and reparameterized Nesterov convention matched by this optimizer.
